WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.090 --> 00:00:01.590
Hi there, Steve Kaufmann here.

00:00:01.860 --> 00:00:08.850
Today I want to give you some
advice, uh, as regards using slang.

00:00:09.900 --> 00:00:19.110
Now, my advice upfront is don't try to
learn slang, or at least don't try, don't

00:00:19.110 --> 00:00:21.000
try to deliberately learn the slang.

00:00:21.000 --> 00:00:23.460
Don't use slang until you are ready.

00:00:24.195 --> 00:00:25.875
Slang is difficult.

00:00:26.655 --> 00:00:31.695
Uh, it's very easy to grab a hold of
some slang terms and throw them into your

00:00:32.174 --> 00:00:34.275
language hoping that they're appropriate.

00:00:34.275 --> 00:00:38.835
I'm hoping that it'll create a
great impression, but it's very,

00:00:38.925 --> 00:00:40.394
very difficult to pull off.

00:00:40.394 --> 00:00:44.985
Now, I know some of you will
immediately say, well, Steve is old.

00:00:45.345 --> 00:00:46.605
What does he know?

00:00:46.995 --> 00:00:49.004
Uh, young people like to use slang.

00:00:49.665 --> 00:00:50.114
Maybe.

00:00:50.670 --> 00:00:53.339
But I can tell you that
it's difficult to use.

00:00:53.519 --> 00:00:55.290
It's constantly changing.

00:00:55.500 --> 00:01:01.830
What's, you know, modern slang today
may be out of fashion by the time you

00:01:01.830 --> 00:01:03.960
find that slang term and try to use it.

00:01:04.739 --> 00:01:10.320
Um, we, if you spend a lot of time
with people who are using slang and

00:01:10.320 --> 00:01:15.810
it becomes sort of very much a part
of your language, then at some point

00:01:15.810 --> 00:01:18.060
you will start naturally using it.

00:01:18.480 --> 00:01:23.250
If on the other hand you get a dictionary
of slang or a list of popular slang

00:01:23.250 --> 00:01:31.440
words in Colombia or in Ecuador or you
know, or in Germany or whatever language

00:01:31.440 --> 00:01:35.370
you're learning, and you say, okay, I'm
gonna use those words and that's gonna

00:01:35.370 --> 00:01:40.440
make me sound more authentic most of the
time, it won't make you sound authentic.

00:01:40.680 --> 00:01:43.620
It'll make, it'll somehow
to the native speaker comes

00:01:43.620 --> 00:01:45.735
across, comes across as strange.

00:01:46.095 --> 00:01:51.495
In other words, there is a point in
our use of language where we naturally

00:01:51.495 --> 00:01:56.235
start to use words that we have heard,
and we have heard them so often that

00:01:56.240 --> 00:01:59.835
we know exactly what they mean and
we know exactly how they're used.

00:02:00.015 --> 00:02:06.255
Now, the same can be said for other words,
until you've heard particular words or

00:02:06.255 --> 00:02:11.085
phrases often enough, you're not totally
confident that you can use them correctly.

00:02:11.445 --> 00:02:16.829
All of that is true, but it's a bigger
issue with slang because with slang we're

00:02:16.829 --> 00:02:23.040
almost saying, you know, I'm totally
cool and up on the latest of slang, and

00:02:23.040 --> 00:02:29.085
you sort of present yourself as someone
who naturally uses these slang terms.

00:02:29.385 --> 00:02:35.655
But if you aren't in fact a person who
naturally uses those slang terms, then

00:02:35.745 --> 00:02:38.055
the impression created is often negative.

00:02:38.055 --> 00:02:41.325
You may get some laughs, but it
doesn't give the impression that you

00:02:41.325 --> 00:02:44.235
are a competent user of the language.

00:02:44.535 --> 00:02:50.430
And by slang, I, I, you know, refer
to sort of terms, uh, you know,

00:02:50.430 --> 00:02:55.140
terms, you know, "Hey dude", you
can say, dude, but if you don't

00:02:55.140 --> 00:02:58.920
feel totally comfortable calling
someone, dude, don't call them dude.

00:02:59.400 --> 00:03:01.590
Uh, comes a point...

00:03:01.590 --> 00:03:04.799
there will be a point where you're
comfortable using that term, but until

00:03:04.799 --> 00:03:06.390
then, and it'll come out naturally.

00:03:06.690 --> 00:03:09.450
It's like so much in language
learning, even, you know,

00:03:09.450 --> 00:03:11.250
polite language in, in Japanese.

00:03:11.250 --> 00:03:16.815
I don't make a deliberate attempt to
learn polite forms so that at some point

00:03:16.815 --> 00:03:18.645
I'm gonna trot out this polite form.

00:03:18.915 --> 00:03:23.505
I sort of speak a neutral form of the
language, and at some point I will have

00:03:23.510 --> 00:03:29.505
heard these polite terms so often that
it'll just be natural for me to use the

00:03:29.505 --> 00:03:32.670
more polite form in a certain situation.

00:03:32.940 --> 00:03:37.920
However, most of the time, if I use the
sort of a neutral form of the language

00:03:38.100 --> 00:03:44.040
that works, nobody in Japan is ever
offended if you use the literal form.

00:03:44.280 --> 00:03:49.680
However, if you use a too familiar,
slangy form of the language with someone

00:03:49.680 --> 00:03:55.800
who is not, say, if you are in group,
that can create a bit of a wincing, uh,

00:03:55.800 --> 00:03:57.150
on the part of the person who hears it.

00:03:57.269 --> 00:04:01.455
Whereas if you stay with neutral
language, you can't go wrong.

00:04:01.635 --> 00:04:05.965
Now, as with so many things in
language, it depends on the language

00:04:05.970 --> 00:04:07.725
environment that you are a part of.

00:04:07.995 --> 00:04:11.415
It's a bit like this, you know,
deliberately learning, you know,

00:04:11.420 --> 00:04:13.815
business English or academic English.

00:04:14.700 --> 00:04:19.560
Uh, slang in many ways is more difficult
than business English or academic English.

00:04:19.620 --> 00:04:25.140
You have to have a very, you know,
strong sense of the sort of sensitivity

00:04:25.145 --> 00:04:29.940
of when to use certain slang words and
when not, or even certain abbreviations.

00:04:30.270 --> 00:04:36.910
There's a tendency because you hear, I'm
gonna wanna you exaggerate gonna  wanna.

00:04:36.930 --> 00:04:40.350
You think the thing to say is, I'm gonna
do this, I'm gonna, I wanna do this.

00:04:40.620 --> 00:04:43.470
I say, I wanna, I'm
gonna, I say it naturally.

00:04:43.470 --> 00:04:47.700
I don't deliberately say I, I mean
I try, I think I'm saying I'm going

00:04:47.700 --> 00:04:51.750
to, but I end up saying I'm gonna,
or I wanna, instead of I want to.

00:04:52.275 --> 00:04:57.165
But the learner should train him
or herself to say, I want to, I'm

00:04:57.165 --> 00:05:01.755
going to, and gradually over time
it'll become, I'm gonna wanna to.

00:05:02.055 --> 00:05:08.775
You know, in French, for example ... but
after a while you start naturally saying

00:05:11.385 --> 00:05:15.675
... but at least in your mind you're
saying ... you're trying to stay with

00:05:15.675 --> 00:05:17.535
a sort of neutral form of the language.

00:05:17.540 --> 00:05:23.715
And naturally certain slang expressions,
certain contractions, certain forms

00:05:23.715 --> 00:05:26.235
of the language will creep into
your language because you've heard

00:05:26.235 --> 00:05:30.885
it so often and you're sort of,
you know, instinctively using those

00:05:30.885 --> 00:05:32.355
forms, in which case it's fine.

00:05:32.625 --> 00:05:36.285
But if you deliberately say, boy,
you know, the really cool French

00:05:36.290 --> 00:05:37.875
people, they don't say ... they say

00:05:37.875 --> 00:05:41.265
...
so I'm gonna say ... it'll,
it'll sound funny.

00:05:41.295 --> 00:05:42.255
It won't work.

00:05:43.409 --> 00:05:46.349
All of this is of course even
more true with swear words.

00:05:46.710 --> 00:05:48.840
Swear words are difficult to use.

00:05:49.200 --> 00:05:50.520
Be very careful.

00:05:50.609 --> 00:05:55.590
Make sure you're comfortable with how
these words are used, who will appreciate

00:05:55.594 --> 00:05:59.640
you using them, and probably your
best advice to stay away from them.

00:06:00.120 --> 00:06:04.110
Uh, and I was referring by the way to
business English or academic English.

00:06:04.469 --> 00:06:09.690
Those are again, forms of the language
where because you are, are in an

00:06:09.690 --> 00:06:11.700
environment which is say business.

00:06:11.910 --> 00:06:17.010
So business Japanese, I spent, you
know, most of my Japanese language

00:06:17.015 --> 00:06:20.970
interactions while living in Japan were
with business people and specifically

00:06:21.180 --> 00:06:23.130
relating to the wood business.

00:06:23.190 --> 00:06:27.420
So I became very familiar with
terms and in fact a lot of the

00:06:27.850 --> 00:06:29.220
customers were from the Kansai.

00:06:29.430 --> 00:06:34.470
So all kinds of, sort of,
in other words, Osaka area.

00:06:34.890 --> 00:06:39.120
Um, you know, expressions,
intonations naturally became a

00:06:39.120 --> 00:06:40.980
part of how I speak Japanese.

00:06:41.040 --> 00:06:45.300
I don't, I didn't deliberately go out
to try and sound more like someone from

00:06:45.300 --> 00:06:50.640
Osaka, but people have told me that there
are times when my, uh, Japanese seems

00:06:50.640 --> 00:06:53.100
to reflect a certain Kansai influence.

00:06:53.160 --> 00:06:54.570
It happens naturally.

00:06:54.750 --> 00:06:59.280
So I think we want to allow our
language to develop naturally

00:06:59.310 --> 00:07:03.630
based on who we're talking to, what
subject matter we're dealing with

00:07:04.020 --> 00:07:09.540
and not to try to deliberately learn,
you know, slang or swear words.

00:07:10.020 --> 00:07:14.340
Uh, or as I say, you know, business
Japanese, business French, business.

00:07:14.460 --> 00:07:18.630
If you are with people enough, if you
read enough material, if you listen

00:07:18.630 --> 00:07:22.860
to enough material, uh, which has a
certain form of the language, you will

00:07:22.860 --> 00:07:24.660
naturally start to speak that way.

00:07:24.750 --> 00:07:29.840
And so I would advise people to place
the emphasis on acquiring different

00:07:29.840 --> 00:07:34.609
ways of speaking naturally by putting
yourself in situations where you're

00:07:34.609 --> 00:07:36.650
hearing that form of a language a lot.

00:07:37.590 --> 00:07:44.250
And to avoid deliberately trying to
impress people with, uh, slang or

00:07:44.910 --> 00:07:49.350
your interpretation of how people are
speaking that you think are very cool

00:07:49.355 --> 00:07:53.880
and you would like to be like them,
rather let the process happen naturally.

00:07:53.940 --> 00:07:57.710
Now, I hope that doesn't sound
too much like an old fuddy duddy,

00:07:57.960 --> 00:08:04.165
like, uh, fuddy duddy is probably
a slang, a very outdated slang.

00:08:04.305 --> 00:08:09.075
Maybe now the equivalent would
be boomer or some such term.

00:08:09.435 --> 00:08:12.375
At any rate that those
are my views on slang.

00:08:12.375 --> 00:08:15.885
I hope that was helpful and I'll
leave you a couple of videos that

00:08:15.885 --> 00:08:20.415
talk about using different forms of
the language in different situations.

00:08:20.565 --> 00:08:21.385
Thank you for listening.

